+    // Multi-token prediction is on by default for a model that carries its own head.
+    //
+    // Ornith 1.5 35B-A3B ships a trained NextN/MTP head inside the same GGUF
+    // (`qwen35moe.nextn_predict_layers = 1`, tensors under blk.40), and llama.cpp
+    // can run it against the target model itself - there is no second model to
+    // download and no draft weights to load. It just never runs unless a
+    // speculative type asks for it: `mparams.load_mtp` and the draft context's
+    // LLAMA_CONTEXT_TYPE_MTP are both derived from `params.speculative.types`,
+    // so a server started without `--spec-type` reads the trunk only and spends
+    // one full pass over 21 GB of weights per generated token.
+    //
+    // The profitable depth is set by what an extra VERIFIED position costs, and
+    // on a sparse trunk that is not what it is on a dense one. A verify pass
+    // over k positions on a dense model costs about what one position costs:
+    // the pass streams one fixed weight set and the extra tokens ride along.
+    // On a 256-expert A3B trunk each token routes to its OWN 8 experts, so k
+    // tokens touch up to 8k distinct expert tiles per layer and the pass gets
+    // genuinely wider. Measured on gfx1201, llama-bench -p k, 5 reps, Q4_K_M:
+    //
+    //     positions   1      2      3      4      8
+    //     ms/pass  14.07  16.33  17.25  18.53  24.27
+    //
+    // i.e. ~1.46 ms per extra verified position against a 14.07 ms pass - 10%
+    // of a full pass per node, where the dense sibling of this family pays
+    // 1-2.7%. With E = 1.35 that puts break-even at ~0.29 accepted tokens per
+    // added node, and this head's measured per-position acceptance is
+    // 0.379 / 0.048 / 0.002 - only the first drafted position clears the bar.
+    // Depths 2 and 3 measure -13.8% and -22.6% against stock; depth 1 is +3.2%.
+    //
+    // Server only: llama-perplexity and the other tools keep decoding exactly as
+    // they did, so the correctness gates measure an untouched engine.
